  some big jobs in government she's taken  on some difficult briefs she's done some  big things with them some things which  are challenging like what but in terms  of equalities there's a very very  sensitive debate around that around what  you do with against racism how you make  sure that discrimination isn't around  she went and brought forward the uh the  commission on on racial disparity she's  actually trying to get the root causes  of some of those issues against a huge  amount of opposition but if you look at  kenny compared to say david cameron zero  ministerial uh experience but he was  opposition leader this is the difference  he was elected to leadership he had not  spent a day in ministerial office before  he became prime minister in 2010 he'd  not taken a single decision as a  minister tony blair had not taken a  single day absolutely he's done that i  accept that and i also accept that there  is perhaps some you know sexism in the  way that we talk about a woman uh  compared to the experience of  david cameron george osborne tony blair  but but the question is the fact that  this is literally going into the job of  prime minister absolutely and as i say  lots of ministerial experience has more  ministerial experience than some of the  people who by common consent have been  very big figures in our political system  over the past 20 years and i think you  know the public are looking for  something different so if you are  looking for something different what  kemi offers is both the ability to  change the ability to say actually we've  done some things with lots of things  well over the last 12 years but we need  to do something different now  we have some experience what  specifically would she cut well she's  going to set some more out of that in  the coming in the coming days but she's  already talked about how we need to look  at university courses because there are  some university courses which perhaps  don't require public subsidy she's  talked